WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.400
Hello, I want to talk today about graph based simultaneous localization mapping or graph

00:06.400 --> 00:12.040
based SLAM and introduce a so called least squares approach to the SLAM problem using

00:12.040 --> 00:16.320
something which is often referred to as post graphs.

00:16.320 --> 00:21.480
So this is a popular technique, which you find today in most state of the art systems

00:21.480 --> 00:27.160
out there that are designed to build a map of the environment and localize a platform

00:27.160 --> 00:28.160
within that map.

00:28.320 --> 00:32.400
And we're looking here to a special form of that, which is the so called post graph based

00:32.400 --> 00:38.160
approach that is an approach which doesn't use landmarks but reduces everything to the

00:38.160 --> 00:39.160
poses.

00:39.160 --> 00:43.600
So everything is kind of reduced to the poses or landmarks are marginalized out.

00:43.600 --> 00:50.120
This leads to a graph structure, which is actually rather easy to maintain and is actually

00:50.120 --> 00:54.720
very flexible also in terms of the observations that you're including and therefore became

00:54.920 --> 01:00.360
one of the standard tools when doing lighter based SLAM for example or working with RGBD

01:00.360 --> 01:02.160
cameras or similar.

01:02.160 --> 01:07.200
So most of those systems which build maps of the environment which have a certain size

01:07.200 --> 01:12.660
which are not let's say a single room or something like this typically use a graph based or post

01:12.660 --> 01:18.880
graph based SLAM approach in the background in order to correct the poses and build a

01:18.880 --> 01:20.720
consistent map of the environment.

01:20.720 --> 01:25.440
And the goal of this lecture today is to introduce you to these graph based SLAM systems

01:25.440 --> 01:30.760
so that you are in the end able to build your own system that performs the optimizations

01:30.760 --> 01:32.360
in here.

01:32.360 --> 01:37.320
And what we're using in order to do this is the technique called least squares that we

01:37.320 --> 01:40.320
just have introduced in the previous lecture.

01:40.320 --> 01:45.680
And so I assume you know how these squares work and at least in a very basic setup.

01:45.680 --> 01:52.440
So Gauss-Newton is something that you should be able to explain or implement and with this

01:52.440 --> 01:58.840
tool at hand and with knowing how ICP works, or at least what the output of ICP is, you

01:58.840 --> 02:01.040
should be able to understand this lecture today.

02:01.040 --> 02:05.520
So within the typical SLAM paradigms we are here in the graph based SLAM approach, which

02:05.520 --> 02:10.640
is, as I said, over the last years became the most popular technique and it basically

02:10.640 --> 02:13.000
is a least squares approach to SLAM.

02:13.000 --> 02:18.280
Of course, there are extensions using different error functions, robustified kernels and things

02:18.280 --> 02:22.880
like this, but we are sticking here with the basics, which should allow you to understand

02:22.880 --> 02:25.920
how this graph based SLAM actually works.

02:25.920 --> 02:30.000
So least squares, again, was an approach for computing a solution of an overdetermined

02:30.000 --> 02:31.520
system.

02:31.520 --> 02:35.740
Overdetermined means here that we have more equations than unknowns.

02:35.740 --> 02:39.800
Unknowns in our example, our parameters, where the robot is in the environment, which will

02:39.800 --> 02:46.160
generate us this post graph and the equations stem from our constraints and constraints

02:46.160 --> 02:49.440
can result from observations or from control commands.

02:49.440 --> 02:55.360
And our goal is to find a configuration for those poses, for that post graph that minimizes

02:55.360 --> 03:01.480
the sum of the squared errors that are introduced by those equations, so by the observations.

03:01.480 --> 03:09.640
So that we have a configuration of these parameters or of these nodes in our graph that allows

03:09.640 --> 03:15.120
us to estimate where the robot is so that the current configuration is as good as possible

03:15.120 --> 03:17.920
in line with what we have seen or experienced.

03:17.920 --> 03:22.720
And again, this least squares approach we apply today to this SLAM problem.

03:22.720 --> 03:25.440
So let me very easily introduce this problem.

03:25.440 --> 03:30.480
So consider we have a mobile robot like this guy here driving around through the environment

03:30.480 --> 03:35.320
and at distinct points in time, let's say every second, for example, we are generating

03:35.320 --> 03:40.160
a pose and the pose is here explained or illustrated by those triangles.

03:40.160 --> 03:44.300
So the robot moved through the environment and while it is moving through the environment,

03:44.300 --> 03:49.200
let's say every second or whenever the robot moved more than three meters or five meters,

03:49.200 --> 03:53.080
that doesn't really matter here, we create a pose or a variable.

03:53.080 --> 03:57.260
And this variable describes the pose of a platform at that point in time.

03:57.260 --> 04:02.680
Pose refers here to the XY or XYZ location plus the heading information.

04:02.680 --> 04:08.780
So XY and the orientation theta in the 2D case or the opposite roll angles, for example,

04:08.780 --> 04:10.680
in the three-dimensional case.

04:10.680 --> 04:14.560
And while the robot is driving through the environment, it creates these poses in this

04:14.560 --> 04:18.640
so-called pose graph and between those poses, they are constraints.

04:18.640 --> 04:22.240
And these constraints result from the motion of the platform.

04:22.240 --> 04:25.440
So for example, you have a robot which counts the revolution of the wheels.

04:25.440 --> 04:29.460
Let's say it counts, often have the wheels been turned and this allows the platform to

04:29.460 --> 04:31.400
estimate its poses.

04:31.400 --> 04:35.520
So let's say I'm the robot right now here, I'm creating a node at the current point in

04:35.520 --> 04:39.320
time, so I'm driving around, I'm turning my wheels, turning my wheels for a certain amount

04:39.320 --> 04:44.880
of time, and then I'm standing here and creating a new pose, a new variable.

04:44.880 --> 04:49.120
And then there exists a constraint between the current pose where I am and the pose where

04:49.120 --> 04:56.520
I have been before, and this constraint results in how many revolutions did the wheels make.

04:56.520 --> 05:00.800
And so if there were, because the revolution of the wheels are related to the distance,

05:00.800 --> 05:03.240
for example, between those two nodes.

05:03.240 --> 05:08.320
So this allows you to say, okay, pose two is, for example, a meter away from pose one.

05:08.320 --> 05:14.800
You're typically uncertain about this information because maybe it's a meter and two centimeters

05:14.800 --> 05:20.000
or 95 centimeters or a meter 20 or a little bit more to the right or to the left.

05:20.000 --> 05:23.940
So you see there are some uncertainties because this information is not perfect.

05:23.940 --> 05:29.440
So see those constraints as kind of soft constraints which say, okay, pose two should be approximatively

05:29.440 --> 05:33.820
a meter away from pose number one, and I think you kind of get the information here.

05:33.820 --> 05:39.020
So the robot drives around, creates poses after different points in time or after things

05:39.020 --> 05:43.560
has traveled a certain distance, and then we have those constraints, and those constraints

05:43.560 --> 05:48.360
can stem from the odometry information or the robot may even use a sensor, for example,

05:48.360 --> 05:53.320
scan matching or ICP aligning consecutive scans in order to estimate its pose.

05:53.320 --> 05:56.500
So this can generate this type of structure that we see here.

05:56.500 --> 05:59.820
So now the robot continues moving through the environment at some point in time.

05:59.820 --> 06:01.920
It revisits a previously seen place.

06:01.920 --> 06:05.020
So it comes back to the same area over here.

06:05.020 --> 06:08.980
And this is one key element in the context of the SLAM problem, something we often refer

06:08.980 --> 06:10.220
to as loop closing.

06:10.220 --> 06:15.640
So the robot comes back to a previously seen place along its trajectory, and at that point

06:15.640 --> 06:20.140
in time, it can actually not create only these sequential constraints but can also generate

06:20.140 --> 06:22.980
those constraints here to previous poses.

06:22.980 --> 06:24.420
How does that work?

06:24.420 --> 06:27.780
So this can work, for example, through an ICP approach.

06:27.780 --> 06:32.900
So we have introduced ICP before where we take, for example, 3D laser scans of the platform

06:32.900 --> 06:39.360
taken at a previous point in time, so taken here or here, and align them with the scan

06:39.360 --> 06:40.700
taken from this location.

06:40.700 --> 06:44.700
And through the alignment of the scans, we can actually compute the relative transformation

06:44.700 --> 06:48.740
between those nodes, something I was in the last lecture on the ICP lecture referring

06:48.740 --> 06:51.940
to as virtual constraints or virtual observations.

06:51.980 --> 06:56.900
And this is exactly those edges over here, or those constraints, soft constraints over

06:56.900 --> 06:57.900
here.

06:57.900 --> 07:04.500
So by revisiting previously visited or places where we've been before or reobserving previously

07:04.500 --> 07:09.700
seen areas, we can generate these constraints between non-successive poses, these kind of

07:09.700 --> 07:11.960
loop closer, so to say.

07:11.960 --> 07:15.060
And so while the robot's driving through the environment, we are getting this kind of graph

07:15.060 --> 07:16.060
structure.

07:16.060 --> 07:19.220
So it's not a kind of chain of poses or a list as it was before, it's really a graph

07:19.220 --> 07:22.140
structure that we now get here.

07:22.140 --> 07:28.660
And this is what gave the name pose graph because we have a graph that consists of poses

07:28.660 --> 07:30.340
of these triangles.

07:30.340 --> 07:35.060
And so this graph is very central to the SLAM problem that we also call it the graph-based

07:35.060 --> 07:39.280
SLAM problem, and we use it to represent our problem here.

07:39.280 --> 07:44.420
So this type of graph is the representation for our SLAM system.

07:44.460 --> 07:50.020
So everything in the graph that we have in there is important for the SLAM problem.

07:50.020 --> 07:54.740
We will use this information in order to compute a solution.

07:54.740 --> 07:58.340
So the graph consists of two things, nodes and edges.

07:58.340 --> 08:03.380
Every node in this graph represents the pose of the robot at a certain point in time.

08:03.380 --> 08:09.220
Again, it's typically a three or a six dimensional vector, depending if we are modeling a robot

08:09.220 --> 08:11.580
in the 2D space or in 3D.

08:11.580 --> 08:16.260
And then we have the edges, and the edges are kind of the relations between those poses.

08:16.260 --> 08:20.800
So they tell me, oh, this pose should be related to the other pose in that certain way.

08:20.800 --> 08:25.460
And this information can come from odometry information or from my sensor observation.

08:25.460 --> 08:28.540
For example, performing scan matching.

08:28.540 --> 08:36.740
So I can say the pose number 10 is related to pose number 11 by being one meter away.

08:36.740 --> 08:41.540
And at the same point in time, you can say the pose number 10 can also be related to

08:41.540 --> 08:44.820
pose number 20 in a certain distance.

08:44.820 --> 08:53.000
And so this information is then being used in the optimization problem to formulate these

08:53.000 --> 08:55.820
equations that are used for the constraints.

08:55.820 --> 09:00.460
So every edge is connected to two poses and relates those two poses, either coming from

09:00.460 --> 09:04.840
odometry information or from my sensor observation, such as ICP.

09:04.840 --> 09:07.220
And in graph-based SLAM, we are building up that graph.

09:07.220 --> 09:11.800
So we build up this full graph of poses and constraints between poses.

09:11.800 --> 09:15.520
And then the goal is to change the location of the poses.

09:15.520 --> 09:19.920
So we're basically shifting and rotating those poses in the pose graph around so that we

09:19.920 --> 09:25.400
minimize the overall error or the squared error that is introduced by those constraints.

09:25.400 --> 09:26.980
So those constraints can be conflicting.

09:26.980 --> 09:32.040
For example, if I'm here and saying the next pose should be one meter away from that pose.

09:32.040 --> 09:35.460
And then I'm driving around, revisiting the environment, and come back to a previously

09:35.460 --> 09:37.520
seen location.

09:37.520 --> 09:44.200
And then, based on this observation, which comes from relating those poses, this pose

09:44.200 --> 09:47.400
should be shifted a little bit.

09:47.400 --> 09:51.320
And then I have one constraint which says they should be a meter away and the other

09:51.320 --> 09:53.640
one should be shifted in a certain way.

09:53.640 --> 09:57.780
And this leads to a fact that those constraints are conflicting.

09:57.780 --> 10:02.260
And I want to find the configuration of the nodes that minimizes the errors introduced

10:02.260 --> 10:05.700
by the constraints.

10:05.700 --> 10:09.740
So we just have also an example here how that looks in reality.

10:09.740 --> 10:15.900
So if we have a robot driving through the environment, so this was a map built by Patrick

10:15.900 --> 10:16.900
Pfaff.

10:16.900 --> 10:22.100
And what you see here is a map built from a laser scanner, where a 2D laser scanner

10:22.100 --> 10:25.700
moving through the environment, and this just encodes the odometry information.

10:25.700 --> 10:28.940
So just estimating how the robot moved based on the revolution of the wheels.

10:28.940 --> 10:31.660
And you can see, actually, this map is extremely messy.

10:31.660 --> 10:33.020
You can't even deal with that.

10:33.020 --> 10:37.020
So you can see these are always the same rooms mapped at different locations.

10:37.020 --> 10:41.040
So odometry itself is typically not sufficient to build a good map.

10:41.040 --> 10:44.780
But what we can do now, we can actually generate this pose graph in this map where we have

10:44.780 --> 10:48.780
been and also relate all those constraints with each other.

10:48.780 --> 10:50.440
So we get something like this.

10:50.440 --> 10:55.060
So you see those lines over here which represent the poses and also represent the constraints.

10:55.060 --> 10:57.780
So that seems, oh, this pose should actually be over here.

10:57.780 --> 10:59.480
This pose should actually be over here.

10:59.480 --> 11:01.660
This pose should actually be over there.

11:01.660 --> 11:05.780
And so these are those constraints, and we now have a graph of all these constraints.

11:05.780 --> 11:10.740
And now we want to find a new configuration of those nodes so that the error is minimized

11:10.740 --> 11:15.540
so that those constraints are actually fulfilled as good as possible.

11:15.540 --> 11:19.780
So we can, at that point in time, actually forget the map, which just was depicted in

11:19.780 --> 11:20.980
the background.

11:20.980 --> 11:27.180
And we are only interested in actually finding new configuration of those nodes because those

11:27.180 --> 11:28.660
nodes correspond to the poses.

11:28.660 --> 11:34.500
And once we have the poses, we can actually build a map just based on the corrected pose

11:34.500 --> 11:35.500
information.

11:35.500 --> 11:38.980
Again, mapping was easy once we know the poses.

11:38.980 --> 11:43.380
So what the graph-based learning approach does, it takes this thing here as an input,

11:43.380 --> 11:48.880
this graph structure, and performs a least square minimization so that it finds new configurations

11:48.880 --> 11:52.420
of the poses that minimize the errors introduced by the constraints.

11:52.420 --> 11:57.600
And this will lead then to a map that looks like this, where all the trajectories are

11:57.600 --> 12:02.480
kind of now overlaid with each other, and the quadratic error introduced by those constraints

12:02.480 --> 12:08.040
is minimized so that when I take this pose information and then render a map, just performing

12:08.040 --> 12:12.140
basically mapping with non-posals, building an occupancy grid map, I will actually get

12:12.140 --> 12:14.220
information which looks like this.

12:14.220 --> 12:19.300
And this is now a consistent map of the environment, so you can see here also this room is always

12:19.300 --> 12:22.980
at the same place and not spread all over the place.

12:22.980 --> 12:29.580
And this process of turning this graph into this graph, this is what we are talking about,

12:29.580 --> 12:33.860
performing the error minimization so to correct the trajectory of the platform that we then

12:33.860 --> 12:39.140
can use this trajectory to build a map of the environment.

12:39.140 --> 12:44.860
Okay, so I have to tell you that this is only one part of the SLAM problem.

12:44.860 --> 12:48.980
This is what we typically call the backend or the optimization part of that.

12:48.980 --> 12:53.060
In reality, of course, there is more involved in this if you want to build a real-world

12:53.060 --> 12:58.100
SLAM system, because you need to turn your observations into constraints, and this is

12:58.100 --> 13:00.420
something we typically refer to as the frontend.

13:00.420 --> 13:04.060
So taking the raw sensor data and turning the sensor data into constraints is kind of

13:04.060 --> 13:08.700
the frontend, and taking those constraints and optimizing the graph and returning the

13:08.700 --> 13:12.060
corrected poses is what's often referred to as the backend.

13:12.060 --> 13:15.040
And in reality, this is an interplay between those two.

13:15.040 --> 13:20.100
So the frontend relates both with each other, then it's being optimized, then this optimized

13:20.100 --> 13:22.700
map information is used to make new data stations.

13:22.700 --> 13:28.380
So in reality, there is actually interplay between these frontend and backend thing.

13:28.380 --> 13:33.060
But here, we are only looking into the backend parts, into the optimization part, and you

13:33.060 --> 13:37.340
can envision the frontend part basically as the ICP algorithm that we've introduced.

13:37.380 --> 13:43.900
ICP is typically used for laser-based SLAM systems as a frontend to generate those constraints,

13:43.900 --> 13:46.320
and the constraint is here actually the relative transformation.

13:46.320 --> 13:53.340
So the outcome of the ICP algorithm, this rotation matrix and this translation vector,

13:53.340 --> 13:57.740
and they are basically forming a constraint together with some uncertainty information,

13:57.740 --> 14:01.800
and there's a little bit more to that than just vanilla ICP in order to do that well.

14:01.800 --> 14:07.680
But for the moment, you can imagine we are running an ICP based on laser scanner, for

14:07.680 --> 14:11.020
example, which gives us the relative transformation between poses.

14:11.020 --> 14:16.720
This is kind of this frontend part here, and we are using this raw data or this constraint

14:16.720 --> 14:24.400
data in order to build the graph or to represent the graph and optimize that graph.

14:24.400 --> 14:30.320
So the graph consists of nodes, so the individual triangles here, these blue triangles, every

14:30.360 --> 14:36.840
of those triangles represents one node, X1, X2, X3, so there's X1, X2, X3, X4, X5, and

14:36.840 --> 14:38.040
so on.

14:38.040 --> 14:45.400
And so the overall graph represents all N nodes, represents X, and this is simply all

14:45.400 --> 14:48.080
nodes put together or stacked on top of each other.

14:48.080 --> 14:53.900
So each Xi is a pose of the robot at time Ti, so at a certain timestamp.

14:53.900 --> 14:57.320
So we assume to have unique timestamps being here.

14:57.320 --> 15:04.280
And if we have a constraint between two poses, from node Xi to node Xj, then this is a constraint

15:04.280 --> 15:08.080
that can result from odometry information or from these virtual observations, and these

15:08.080 --> 15:11.240
are those edges over here.

15:11.240 --> 15:16.080
And we will now look a little bit into that, how we actually describe those edges, and

15:16.080 --> 15:22.440
then once we have those edges and can interpret them, then we see how we can actually perform

15:22.440 --> 15:27.800
the error minimization task using the idea of least squares, or in this case Gauss-Newton.

15:27.800 --> 15:35.440
Okay, so an edge exists between two nodes if the robot moves from Xi to Xi plus one.

15:35.440 --> 15:40.960
So there's now a consecutive edge, and this is an edge which can be generated from odometry

15:40.960 --> 15:47.000
or also from incremental ICP, so matching the current skin against the next skin.

15:47.000 --> 15:50.920
But for simplicity reasons, just assume right now it's odometry, so just by counting the

15:50.920 --> 15:52.560
revolution of the wheels.

15:52.560 --> 15:54.760
So we are here, the robot is currently here.

15:54.760 --> 16:00.280
It moves forward, and then based on the odometry information, it can estimate where it should

16:00.280 --> 16:06.760
be given the motion follows the kinematic model that the robot has about itself.

16:06.760 --> 16:12.160
And this is basically the information, like a motion model that we're using.

16:12.160 --> 16:18.080
So if you think about the previous lectures, this is basically counting the translational

16:18.080 --> 16:22.000
velocity or the rotational velocity based on your steering, such as differential drive

16:22.000 --> 16:26.520
or Ackermann steering, and that tells you how much you should have moved if everything

16:26.520 --> 16:27.680
looks good.

16:27.680 --> 16:31.680
And from probabilistic point of view, that's basically a mean estimate of where you have

16:31.680 --> 16:37.880
been moving within the time difference from I to I plus one, and this typically comes

16:37.880 --> 16:39.560
from odometry information.

16:39.560 --> 16:47.840
So this is a transformation typically which tells us how Xi can be seen, or how Xi plus

16:47.840 --> 16:49.600
one can be seen from Xi.

16:49.600 --> 16:53.760
So this is a transformation, so how do you transfer one pose and the other pose?

16:53.760 --> 16:56.840
So relative pose information.

16:56.840 --> 17:02.480
An edge can also exist between two arbitrary poses, so not I and I plus one, but I and

17:02.480 --> 17:09.720
J, where J is different from I plus one, and then we typically need to relate this information

17:09.720 --> 17:11.960
with each other using the sensor data.

17:11.960 --> 17:16.460
So as an example, so consider this now as a 2D illustration, so we have our robot at

17:16.460 --> 17:21.300
Xi sitting over here, and this is the scene it is observing with the laser scanner, and

17:21.300 --> 17:25.640
then at a position XJ it's here, and it's observing this part.

17:25.640 --> 17:32.100
So by relating these black lines and this blue line, treating this as a scan, and overlaying

17:32.100 --> 17:37.740
them with each other, we can actually generate kind of a virtual observation by saying we

17:37.740 --> 17:44.820
can have an idea where XJ should be as seen from Xi, although we can't really see XJ,

17:44.820 --> 17:48.380
we can't really see where the robot is in the future.

17:48.380 --> 17:53.140
But by relating it to the environment that we are seeing from both locations, we can

17:53.140 --> 17:56.940
actually generate something which we call a virtual measurement.

17:56.940 --> 18:02.580
It's nothing else than the result of the iterative closest point algorithm in general.

18:02.580 --> 18:09.120
So again, we see this kind of a treat, this is our reference frame, and by matching the

18:09.120 --> 18:15.220
black line and the blue line, we are basically moving the blue one over here, and then if

18:15.220 --> 18:20.440
we know where the blue one is, we also know where Xi is in this reference frame of XJ.

18:20.440 --> 18:27.000
So by constructing this kind of virtual measurement through an alignment procedure such as ICP,

18:27.000 --> 18:32.960
or if you think about vision, this could be competing with relative orientation, for example

18:32.960 --> 18:36.680
you can use the five-point algorithm or the eight-point algorithm for image features,

18:36.680 --> 18:41.580
but we are restricting ourselves here for the simplicity to ICP, then we would get this

18:41.580 --> 18:42.580
type of alignment.

18:42.580 --> 18:46.240
So you see here the black and the blue lines are aligned on top of each other, and this

18:46.240 --> 18:49.620
allows us to relate the poses Xi and XJ with each other.

18:49.620 --> 18:57.240
And then this transformation from Xi to XJ can then be seen as a virtual measurement

18:57.240 --> 19:01.960
about the position XJ as it would be seen from Xi.

19:01.960 --> 19:07.600
So if Xi could see the robot at XJ, it would be the pose of the robot relative to its own

19:07.600 --> 19:13.480
pose, but as we can physically not see that, we do this through the relation of the environment.

19:13.480 --> 19:16.440
And again, this is a full transformation between those two poses.

19:16.440 --> 19:22.240
So it's not just kind of a distance vector or something like this, it's a full transformation

19:22.240 --> 19:29.440
that we can transform the pose Xi to the pose XJ or the other way around.

19:29.440 --> 19:32.400
So how do we encode those transformations?

19:32.400 --> 19:38.600
We typically use things like homogeneous coordinates for doing this, or there are other ways in

19:38.600 --> 19:42.280
which you can do this, but at least for formulating it and writing it down in here, I will be

19:42.280 --> 19:46.440
using homogeneous coordinates because this is actually very easy to do.

19:46.440 --> 19:54.080
So just as a reminder, homogeneous coordinates allow you to express or to chain transformations

19:54.080 --> 19:58.400
like translations and rotation in a very elegant way.

19:58.400 --> 20:01.760
And then everything which is here written in capitalized characters, you should see

20:01.760 --> 20:02.800
as a transformation.

20:02.800 --> 20:11.380
So this is kind of the transformation basically of the pose Xi and seen as a translation and

20:11.380 --> 20:14.840
a rotation in a global reference frame.

20:14.840 --> 20:16.720
So this is a homogeneous matrix.

20:16.720 --> 20:20.360
This is also homogeneous matrix representing this pose Xi and XJ.

20:20.360 --> 20:27.160
And if I have those matrices, I can here invert the matrix Xi and multiply the matrix Xi plus

20:27.160 --> 20:30.440
one to it, then this is the relative transformation.

20:30.440 --> 20:34.880
Where is Xi plus one as seen from Xi?

20:34.880 --> 20:46.960
And the same holds here, Xi inverse XJ means the node, how the node I sees node J, so how

20:46.960 --> 20:49.920
J would be seen from node I.

20:49.920 --> 20:54.800
So just as a very short reminder in case you have forgotten about homogeneous coordinates,

20:54.800 --> 21:00.360
just a few words how what that is, homogeneous coordinates are a different coordinate system

21:00.360 --> 21:04.320
or coordinates which are typically used in projective geometry.

21:04.320 --> 21:09.480
It has some nice advantages of being able, for example, to express points which are infinitively

21:09.480 --> 21:13.600
far away in those things, but that's not kind of the reason why we are using it here in

21:13.600 --> 21:14.600
the lecture.

21:14.600 --> 21:17.880
We're using it here in the lecture because in this homogeneous coordinates, a single

21:17.880 --> 21:23.640
matrix can represent all fine or even projective transformations.

21:23.960 --> 21:30.000
That's attractive for us because we will be using here rotations and translations, so

21:30.000 --> 21:35.320
rigid body transformations, which are a subset of those two transformations, through matrices.

21:35.320 --> 21:39.720
And so that means we have a matrix that represents a translation and we have a matrix which represents

21:39.720 --> 21:44.680
a rotation, and we can just multiply those matrices in order to change the transformation

21:44.680 --> 21:45.760
after each other.

21:45.760 --> 21:49.480
And that makes it very elegant in order to write it down, and this is one of the advantages

21:49.480 --> 21:53.720
of homogeneous coordinates, that they typically simplify the mass, at least for us, when we

21:53.720 --> 21:55.680
need to write that down.

21:55.680 --> 21:56.680
So how does it work?

21:56.680 --> 21:59.960
I just have a short example, and if you need more information about this, I recommend you

21:59.960 --> 22:02.800
to watch my lecture on homogeneous coordinates.

22:02.800 --> 22:06.440
But just as a very short reminder, if you want to represent the n-dimensional space

22:06.440 --> 22:11.160
like the three-dimensional space for us, then homogeneous coordinates use one dimension

22:11.160 --> 22:13.240
more, so n plus one-dimensional space.

22:13.240 --> 22:17.280
So for us, it would be four dimensions for modeling the three-dimensional space.

22:17.280 --> 22:21.400
And the vector in homogeneous coordinates, let's say x, y, that, is extended by a new

22:21.400 --> 22:26.560
dimension x, y, that, and the next dimension, which is one in this example.

22:26.560 --> 22:31.000
There's a mapping from the Euclidean space to homogeneous coordinates.

22:31.000 --> 22:36.840
There's also backward mapping, which we say we have the four coordinates, x, y, z, w in

22:36.840 --> 22:42.320
this example, and then we take the last dimension and divide all other dimensions by that last

22:42.320 --> 22:44.000
dimension and then drop the last one.

22:44.000 --> 22:48.160
So we go from a four-dimensional vector to a three-dimensional vector, dividing all the

22:48.160 --> 22:49.760
elements by w.

22:49.760 --> 22:53.160
And so it's kind of a form of normalization.

22:53.160 --> 22:58.720
And if we do this, this forward-backward mapping, then we can actually express translations

22:58.720 --> 23:00.440
in a rotation-elegant way.

23:00.440 --> 23:06.040
So a translation can then be expressed in a four-dimensional matrix, which has ones

23:06.040 --> 23:07.040
on the main diagonal.

23:07.040 --> 23:11.400
Here, everything is zero, and those three dimensions are the translation x, translation

23:11.400 --> 23:13.440
y, and translation z.

23:13.440 --> 23:17.600
And the rotation can be expressed by a 3D rotation matrix, the standard rotation matrix

23:17.600 --> 23:21.200
as you know them, here in this upper three-by-three block.

23:21.200 --> 23:27.200
And so this is a zero vector, and this is also a zero vector and a one.

23:27.200 --> 23:33.560
And so by having this rotation matrix in homogeneous coordinates and this translation in homogeneous

23:33.560 --> 23:38.080
coordinates, we now can multiply this translation matrix and the rotation matrix with each other

23:38.080 --> 23:42.280
and then have a translation and rotation expressed in one single matrix.

23:42.280 --> 23:48.600
That's something you cannot do in the Euclidean space, but it's very, very elegant for writing

23:48.600 --> 23:50.760
this down and chaining those transformations.

23:50.760 --> 23:57.600
So if you go back, for example, something like this to express this in Euclidean coordinates

23:57.600 --> 23:59.760
is much more difficult.

23:59.760 --> 24:01.380
And here, it's very easy to see.

24:01.380 --> 24:08.200
You take the poles of the transformation, which encodes xi, and you basically invert

24:08.200 --> 24:09.200
it.

24:09.200 --> 24:13.240
So by inverting this four-by-four matrix, you're actually inverting the transformation

24:13.240 --> 24:18.440
and multiplying to that from the right-hand side xj, so what you can see then that this

24:18.440 --> 24:24.640
is actually the node xi as it would be seen from the node xj.

24:24.640 --> 24:27.060
So that's what we are using here.

24:27.060 --> 24:34.000
In addition to this, our constraints are affected by noise because we have observations.

24:34.000 --> 24:39.400
So for example, odometry is typically less accurate than using your sensor's information

24:39.400 --> 24:43.360
if you think about a laser scanner, for example.

24:43.360 --> 24:46.080
But even your laser scanner data is affected by noise.

24:46.080 --> 24:50.120
And so the transformation that you're actually computing are not perfect.

24:50.120 --> 24:56.180
And the information matrix omega ij is the information matrix related to the constraint

24:56.180 --> 25:01.040
between the node i and the node j and encodes the uncertainty that stems from the observation.

25:01.080 --> 25:04.120
So how much do we trust that information?

25:04.120 --> 25:09.880
And we may have a sensor where this is the same for all constraints, but we may have

25:09.880 --> 25:14.160
different types of sensors or different situations in which the sensor is more accurate in a

25:14.160 --> 25:18.280
certain setup and less accurate in another setup, and we can actually use this information.

25:18.280 --> 25:21.900
So the higher the value, the larger the values in this information matrix, the more information

25:21.900 --> 25:24.320
we have, the smaller the uncertainty.

25:24.320 --> 25:30.200
And the bigger it is, this omega or the values inside omega, the more this edge matters in

25:30.240 --> 25:31.240
the optimization.

25:31.240 --> 25:35.760
So if you remember back the least squares, how it worked, then this information matrix

25:35.760 --> 25:39.000
is basically a weighting factor for your error.

25:39.000 --> 25:44.640
So if you have a constraint which has an information matrix which is larger, which has a value

25:44.640 --> 25:52.840
which is twice as large, it will count more twice as much inside the optimization.

25:52.840 --> 25:59.040
So this edge information is actually important in order to take the uncertainty into account.

25:59.040 --> 26:03.440
So then the question is how would you expect this matrix to look like if you compare scan

26:03.440 --> 26:06.920
matching done with ICP to odometry information?

26:06.920 --> 26:10.240
So which is more accurate or less accurate?

26:10.240 --> 26:14.400
That's one relevant information you should be able to figure out.

26:14.400 --> 26:17.960
The other thing which would be relevant if you think about ICP and you make this scan

26:17.960 --> 26:21.960
alignment and you have a robot which drives along a very, very long corridor, and this

26:21.960 --> 26:25.020
corridor is, let's say, very sparse of features.

26:25.020 --> 26:27.680
It's just long walls.

26:27.680 --> 26:31.000
How would this information matrix look like?

26:31.000 --> 26:34.720
And thinking about those questions allows you a little bit to understand if you can

26:34.720 --> 26:39.400
relate your observations and the information you can extract from your observations with

26:39.400 --> 26:42.200
this graph construction here.

26:42.200 --> 26:48.240
So in the first setup, related to the first question, your sensor information, at least

26:48.240 --> 26:52.840
from a laser range scanner, is typically better than the odometry information that you have.

26:52.840 --> 26:57.160
Again, it depends how much engineering you put into your odometry information or your

26:57.160 --> 27:00.600
laser scan information and what you know about the environment, but at least for a

27:00.600 --> 27:04.520
lot of, let's say, standard indoor mapping systems, you would actually trust your laser

27:04.520 --> 27:09.640
scanner quite a bit more, so you expect the covariance matrices to be smaller.

27:09.640 --> 27:13.800
That means the values in the information matrices to be bigger.

27:13.800 --> 27:18.260
This may be different if you have a less accurate scanner or you have a lot of motion or movement

27:18.260 --> 27:22.440
dynamics in the environment, but put a lot of engineering into getting your odometry

27:22.440 --> 27:24.160
information right.

27:24.160 --> 27:27.840
Similarly, having redundant systems, taking multiple vehicles into account, a very good

27:27.840 --> 27:32.480
dynamics model, for example, in case of an autonomous vehicle, autonomous car, you can

27:32.480 --> 27:39.400
invest quite some brainpower into your odometry system that you can trust odometry also more.

27:39.400 --> 27:43.720
Regarding the second question, how would this information matrix look like or the corresponding

27:43.720 --> 27:47.080
covariance matrix look like in a long featureless corridor?

27:47.080 --> 27:48.080
So what does it mean?

27:48.080 --> 27:51.700
A long featureless corridor means the robot can localize very precisely with respect to

27:51.700 --> 27:55.380
its right and left-hand side because you see the walls of the corridor.

27:55.380 --> 27:58.500
So you have a very small uncertainty with respect to your motion to the right and left-hand

27:58.500 --> 27:59.500
side.

27:59.500 --> 28:03.580
Or with respect to your orientation, given that you have very long walls, you can typically

28:03.580 --> 28:06.900
align the scan very good with respect to a corridor.

28:06.900 --> 28:09.940
So you shouldn't get much drift to the left and right-hand side.

28:09.940 --> 28:13.380
But what actually is hard to estimate is where you're moving along that corridor.

28:13.380 --> 28:17.460
So you have a very high uncertainty along the main axis of the corridor.

28:17.460 --> 28:23.420
So you would expect to get a covariance matrix, which is very narrow to your right-hand side,

28:23.420 --> 28:26.940
but very long and stretched along the main axis of your corridor.

28:26.940 --> 28:34.540
And so if you inspect your covariance matrices of your graph, you can actually see something

28:34.540 --> 28:38.700
about what information did the sensor actually tell you.

28:38.700 --> 28:43.700
And this then is an important information how the uncertainty actually propagates in

28:43.700 --> 28:46.280
the end through your graph through a least squares problem.

28:46.280 --> 28:50.160
Because if you have very large uncertainties along certain dimensions, this will also lead

28:50.160 --> 28:53.560
to a large uncertainty along this line.

28:53.560 --> 28:56.920
Okay, so let's have a look to the postgraph and look to all the information that we have.

28:56.920 --> 28:59.680
So what we see here is a very, very tiny postgraph.

28:59.680 --> 29:04.000
We just have kind of two nodes over here, xi and xj.

29:04.000 --> 29:07.680
So you can see this basically is cutting away everything else.

29:07.680 --> 29:11.640
And what we have is we have a current configuration of the nodes.

29:11.640 --> 29:17.000
And this is those two positions here, just let's say in the 2D plane, just to make it

29:17.000 --> 29:18.760
easy to illustrate that here.

29:18.760 --> 29:23.400
So this is, we think, this node sits over here and that node sits over there.

29:23.400 --> 29:26.420
And there's a current configuration of the graph.

29:26.420 --> 29:31.080
Now we have a constraint between those two because there's a common observation, for

29:31.080 --> 29:33.040
example, from ICP.

29:33.040 --> 29:37.640
And let's say this is what the observation looks like with the corresponding uncertainty

29:37.640 --> 29:40.680
here illustrated through a covariance matrix.

29:40.680 --> 29:48.280
So this is basically, this is the position where if I would sit in xi, would expect xj

29:48.280 --> 29:49.280
to be.

29:49.280 --> 29:53.140
And you can see here that there's actually a mismatch between what the observation tells

29:53.140 --> 29:56.760
me and what the current graph configuration tells me.

29:56.760 --> 30:02.000
So this is an error that we are computing, and this is the error that we actually want

30:02.000 --> 30:04.100
to minimize, okay?

30:04.100 --> 30:10.860
So this thing should be brought to zero over all constraints, of course.

30:10.860 --> 30:17.160
So and this edge here is basically represented by the kind of the mean observation here encoded

30:17.160 --> 30:22.580
as zij and an information matrix, which is here illustrated through the inverse of it,

30:22.580 --> 30:25.060
through the covariance matrix.

30:25.060 --> 30:28.060
And so this is the information we store in every graph.

30:28.060 --> 30:32.960
So every observation will lead to such an edge.

30:32.960 --> 30:36.560
And what we're actually trying to minimize, we want to shift the configuration of those

30:36.560 --> 30:39.200
nodes in order to minimize this error.

30:39.200 --> 30:45.280
So in this case, it means xj should actually move here in order to bring this error towards

30:45.280 --> 30:46.280
zero.

30:46.280 --> 30:49.920
And of course, the problem is it's not just one edge which is involved in this, otherwise

30:49.920 --> 30:52.840
it would be easy to satisfy this constraint.

30:52.840 --> 30:57.160
We have the goal of doing this for all our constraints.

30:57.160 --> 31:00.920
So this was just kind of one example, but of course, the whole graph consists of those

31:00.920 --> 31:01.960
constraints.

31:01.960 --> 31:06.840
And what we want to do is we want to minimize the overall sum of that.

31:06.840 --> 31:10.760
And this is where the least squares approach or the ideas of least squares actually kick

31:10.760 --> 31:11.760
in.

31:11.760 --> 31:17.600
So we can go back to our previous lecture and think, okay, this is now our squared error

31:17.600 --> 31:18.600
function.

31:18.600 --> 31:24.880
We have our error vector relating the constraint i and j with each other, the information matrix

31:24.880 --> 31:30.560
and again, the error term, or we can kind of rewritten this in a short version where

31:30.560 --> 31:34.280
we just put the vector x in here, the whole state vector.

31:34.280 --> 31:37.520
So this was the way we were formulating this before in the previous lecture where we say

31:37.520 --> 31:42.180
the error or the error vector here depends on the state variable x.

31:42.180 --> 31:45.880
What is important now or the difference here in the SLAM problem, we can formulate it in

31:45.880 --> 31:50.960
that way that we say not the whole vector x depends actually on this error term, but

31:50.960 --> 31:53.200
only xi and xj.

31:53.200 --> 31:59.400
That means only the node xi and node xj, so those two contribute to this individual error

31:59.400 --> 32:00.400
term.

32:00.440 --> 32:04.520
All the other state variables do not matter for that single constraint.

32:04.520 --> 32:07.760
And this is something, an information that we will later on exploit in order to solve

32:07.760 --> 32:13.680
the SLAM problem in a very efficient way or in an efficient way because we know that the

32:13.680 --> 32:17.040
individual error terms only depend on a very small number of variables.

32:17.040 --> 32:20.600
And that's something that we will exploit later on.

32:20.600 --> 32:24.280
The first question we may look like, what does the state vector actually look like?

32:24.280 --> 32:27.040
So you now want to sit down and actually implement our system.

32:27.040 --> 32:30.400
How should that actually look like?

32:30.400 --> 32:39.120
And what we need is our state vector x is simply the concatenation of the poses of the

32:39.120 --> 32:40.120
graph.

32:40.120 --> 32:47.220
So every vector, so one vector for every node of the graph, and we just kind of concatenate

32:47.220 --> 32:48.220
them with each other.

32:48.220 --> 32:50.560
So it gets a very, very, very long vector.

32:50.560 --> 32:56.120
And it's always kind of x, y, theta, x, y, theta, x, y, theta, it's always the x of the

32:56.120 --> 33:00.840
first node, the y of the first node, the orientation that the robot had when being there, and then

33:00.840 --> 33:03.920
for the second node, x, y, theta, x, y, theta.

33:03.920 --> 33:08.040
Or if you're doing a three-dimensional work, x, y, that, your pitch roll, for example,

33:08.040 --> 33:13.600
or another representation on how we could represent the six-dimension degree of freedom

33:13.600 --> 33:14.600
poses.

33:14.600 --> 33:16.000
Okay, so we have that.

33:16.000 --> 33:17.360
We know what the state vector looks like.

33:17.360 --> 33:20.380
So next thing is let's look into the error function.

33:20.380 --> 33:22.720
How do we relate those error functions with each other?

33:22.720 --> 33:28.360
We said, yes, of course, those, the individual poses x, i, and x, j should pop up, and also

33:28.360 --> 33:31.200
the observation should be taken into account.

33:31.200 --> 33:36.200
So the result of the ICP approach should play a role in here.

33:36.200 --> 33:39.640
And we can formulate a single constraint now in the following way.

33:39.640 --> 33:41.220
So what do we see here?

33:41.220 --> 33:45.040
So let's just look only first to this part over here.

33:45.040 --> 33:46.200
So the first thing.

33:46.200 --> 33:48.440
So at x, i inverse x, j.

33:48.440 --> 33:56.740
So this is kind of how x, i sees x, j, or how x, j is seen if I would, if the center

33:56.740 --> 33:58.960
of the coordinate system would be in x, i.

33:58.960 --> 34:04.120
So you can think of seeing yourself standing in x, i, and then see where x, j actually

34:04.120 --> 34:05.120
looks like.

34:05.120 --> 34:09.240
So this is what this information provides, and it's directly very easily computed through

34:09.240 --> 34:11.300
the homogeneous coordinates.

34:11.300 --> 34:12.800
And then we have an observation.

34:12.800 --> 34:17.280
This observation z tells me how x, i sees x, j.

34:17.280 --> 34:19.460
This is a result of the ICP algorithm.

34:19.460 --> 34:24.160
So the transformation that results from ICP, so the rotation matrix and translation vector.

34:24.160 --> 34:25.160
This is this z.

34:25.160 --> 34:31.760
And then I invert this z and multiply it here from the, to the configuration of the graph.

34:31.760 --> 34:38.240
So the whole block here, you can see where is, what's the difference between the poses

34:38.240 --> 34:42.420
with respect to the graph, and what's the difference of the poses with respect to the

34:42.420 --> 34:43.420
measurement.

34:43.420 --> 34:48.380
And so one is kind of, you can see this as kind of a forward transformation on what the

34:48.380 --> 34:53.080
graph tells me, and then a backward transformation on what the observation tells me.

34:53.080 --> 34:56.440
And then I need to look into the difference between where I started and where I end up,

34:56.440 --> 34:59.180
and this is basically my error function.

34:59.180 --> 35:02.400
So that's what actually happens in this part over here.

35:02.400 --> 35:06.960
So the relative transformation according to the graph in one direction, and then the inverse

35:06.960 --> 35:11.860
of the observation, so turning this around and going back in order to compute the difference

35:11.860 --> 35:12.860
between them.

35:12.860 --> 35:17.500
And then there's a function called t to v over here, a magic function.

35:17.500 --> 35:22.500
T to v means transformation to vector, and v to t means vector to transformation.

35:22.500 --> 35:29.220
So this is basically just a mapping between a vectorized representation vector, so x,

35:29.220 --> 35:35.060
y, theta for example, and the transformation expressed in homogeneous coordinates.

35:35.060 --> 35:44.180
So it's just kind of a forward-backward mapping from this three by three, or from this vector,

35:44.180 --> 35:51.820
three by three homogeneous matrix into a three-dimensional vector with x, y, theta.

35:51.820 --> 35:57.580
So that the transformation consisting of a two-dimensional translation vector and one

35:57.580 --> 36:01.960
rotation angle encoded in a three by three matrix is put into a vector.

36:01.960 --> 36:07.120
And because this here again is a transformation matrix, and we need to put it in vector form

36:07.120 --> 36:11.720
in order to put into our least squares problem, and that's what this t to v stands for.

36:11.720 --> 36:16.920
So this gives us here in this example, if we are doing this in the 2D plane, a three-dimensional

36:16.920 --> 36:21.240
vector with x, y, and one orientation constraint.

36:21.240 --> 36:25.080
Or we can express this also not just for a single constraint, but we could express the

36:25.080 --> 36:29.160
same also for the whole state vector.

36:29.160 --> 36:36.520
And then of course, only those components matter where there is actually an involvement.

36:36.520 --> 36:40.760
And to also be cleared, what happens, when is the error of zero?

36:40.760 --> 36:42.840
When does the error take a value of zero?

36:42.840 --> 36:48.220
And this is exactly the case if my observation is the same than my relative graph configuration.

36:48.220 --> 36:53.120
So if what the graph tells is exactly the same what my observation tells me, then this

36:53.120 --> 36:59.200
equation holds if I would multiply the, and then you can actually see that if I would

36:59.200 --> 37:03.640
multiply here z inverse from the left hand side, so the inverse observation, and this

37:03.640 --> 37:06.560
term is the same, then this is the identity matrix.

37:06.560 --> 37:12.160
So that means if the transformation of, going the transformation through the graph and backwards

37:12.160 --> 37:16.040
through the inverse of the observation gives me the identity matrix, means there is no

37:16.040 --> 37:21.200
transformation mismatch, and this would then give you the identity matrix would be mapped

37:21.200 --> 37:23.800
through this function to zero, zero, zero.

37:23.800 --> 37:27.720
So a zero error vector, okay?

37:27.720 --> 37:33.460
So the only thing we need to specify is how do we get z, and this is something that our

37:33.460 --> 37:38.520
ICP, that's the result of the ICP algorithm, just put into a homogeneous vector.

37:38.520 --> 37:44.860
And these are just the poses in my post graph expressed in homogeneous coordinates.

37:44.860 --> 37:49.260
And with this, we actually can look, okay, let's rebuild Gauss-Newton.

37:49.260 --> 37:52.300
How did the overall error minimization procedure look like?

37:52.300 --> 37:54.900
So the first thing we need to do, define the error function.

37:54.900 --> 37:56.220
That's something that we just did.

37:56.220 --> 38:01.260
What we then need to do, we need to linearize our error function, just because we need to

38:01.260 --> 38:05.900
perform local linearizations, and then this leads to the quadratic form where we compute

38:05.900 --> 38:09.420
the first derivative and set the first derivative to zero.

38:09.420 --> 38:13.060
This gives us a system of linear equations.

38:13.060 --> 38:14.860
We solve the system of linear equations.

38:14.860 --> 38:19.940
We get the update of our state, and then we iterate this process, restarting here the

38:19.940 --> 38:25.880
linearization until we have converged, as we discussed it in the lecture before.

38:25.880 --> 38:32.240
So just as a short reminder, this was our error function, the original error function,

38:32.240 --> 38:34.660
which we now need to linearize.

38:34.660 --> 38:39.360
And again, if you think about this homogeneous transformation matrices, you may remember

38:39.360 --> 38:43.120
in a rotation matrix, there are sine and cosine functions popping up.

38:43.120 --> 38:44.460
These are nonlinear functions.

38:44.460 --> 38:47.300
So you need to perform linearization and linearize that thing.

38:47.300 --> 38:51.700
That's where you need to sit down with a piece of paper and actually try to perform this

38:51.700 --> 38:53.080
linearization.

38:53.080 --> 38:59.740
Or if you use another error minimization framework, you may have some systems to compute this

38:59.740 --> 39:04.180
automatically or numerically for you, depending on how good your tools are, but otherwise

39:04.180 --> 39:05.460
you have to do it by hand.

39:05.460 --> 39:08.220
That means you need to compute a matrix.

39:08.220 --> 39:12.420
This is your Jacobian matrix, and every element of the Jacobian matrix consists of the partial

39:12.420 --> 39:18.600
derivatives of deriving your error function or the individual dimensions of your error

39:18.600 --> 39:23.940
functions with respect to the individual dimensions of your state vector.

39:23.940 --> 39:30.660
So you derive it in this case with respect to x1, x2, x3, and so on and so forth.

39:30.660 --> 39:35.380
And here, we have to look a little bit into the details because how that looks like actually

39:35.380 --> 39:42.420
has a very big impact on how efficiently we can actually solve the state estimation problem

39:42.420 --> 39:43.420
later on.

39:43.420 --> 39:49.780
So the first question we need to answer, does the error term depend on all state variables?

39:49.780 --> 39:51.980
So again, this is an error term eij.

39:51.980 --> 39:55.660
That means for one single observation.

39:55.660 --> 40:00.720
And does this depend on all state variables, on the whole state vector?

40:00.720 --> 40:06.480
It clearly depends on the state vector, but does it depend on all elements in there?

40:06.480 --> 40:11.460
And within the SLAM problem, this is not the case because we said that this error term

40:11.460 --> 40:15.920
only relates the two variables to which this edge is connected in our graph.

40:15.920 --> 40:21.440
So there are just two poses, namely the pose with index i and the pose with index j on

40:21.440 --> 40:23.660
which this actually depends on.

40:23.660 --> 40:29.840
So no, this depends only on xi and xj, and this will play an important role because this

40:29.960 --> 40:37.360
has an impact on the structure of the Jacobian that is involved in this.

40:37.360 --> 40:43.220
You may think about this if you have a function which depends only on a small subset of variables.

40:43.220 --> 40:48.980
If you then derive this function with respect to the variables, then it will have a lot

40:48.980 --> 40:55.360
of zero elements, namely if a function doesn't depend on one variable and you derive with

40:55.360 --> 40:58.300
respect to this variable, it will be zero.

40:58.300 --> 41:04.580
And this is the key insight because this leads to the fact that the Jacobian will actually

41:04.580 --> 41:12.000
be zero nearly everywhere except for the elements which are related to the variable xi and with

41:12.000 --> 41:14.900
respect to the variables xj.

41:14.900 --> 41:22.420
So if you think of, you have a whatever, 10,000 dimensional state vector, and you have a constraint

41:22.420 --> 41:27.820
just between two poses, then only those two poses will be related with each other.

41:27.860 --> 41:32.980
And the all other 10,000 minus six dimensions will actually be zero.

41:32.980 --> 41:37.500
So your Jacobian will be a large vector which consists only of zeros and just has a very

41:37.500 --> 41:41.940
small number of elements which are involved in here.

41:41.940 --> 41:48.540
So the partial derivative in this Jacobian of the arrow function with respect to all

41:48.540 --> 41:56.300
the parameters is basically you have here all your arrow vectors and your state variables

41:56.300 --> 42:01.940
will be zero everywhere except of those blocks which are related to xi and those which are

42:01.940 --> 42:02.940
related to xj.

42:02.940 --> 42:08.460
So in this case, for example, if xi is in 2D plane, it's x, y, and theta, there will

42:08.460 --> 42:13.380
be three elements here which are non-zero, three elements which are here that are non-zero,

42:13.380 --> 42:15.640
and the rest will be completely zero.

42:15.640 --> 42:22.340
So we just have two blocks of variables here, we call them aij, bij, which are non-zero,

42:22.340 --> 42:25.020
and all the rest is zero.

42:25.060 --> 42:29.100
So this is kind of a key insight because this leads to a sparse Jacobian which will also

42:29.100 --> 42:34.220
have a big effect on our H matrix later on.

42:34.220 --> 42:38.020
So Jacobian and sparsity.

42:38.020 --> 42:43.980
If our arrow function depends only on the variables xi and xj, that means all other

42:43.980 --> 42:50.780
elements, which are x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, and so on, which are not i and j, are zero.

42:50.780 --> 42:53.480
And these are illustrated by these blue blocks over here.

42:53.480 --> 42:57.920
So these are all the blocks which are zero, and we just have these small blocks which

42:57.920 --> 43:00.120
are non-zero.

43:00.120 --> 43:06.040
And this is kind of a key insight in here, that we have this huge Jacobian, everything

43:06.040 --> 43:10.720
is zero in this Jacobian except those two blocks, and this is the key insight.

43:10.720 --> 43:15.640
So it's one of the most important slides in this slide set, having this insight, because

43:15.640 --> 43:23.200
this allows you to solve, let's say, real-world SLAM problems in an efficient manner.

43:23.200 --> 43:24.200
Why is this the case?

43:24.200 --> 43:26.120
Why is this so important?

43:26.120 --> 43:32.080
This can be illustrated by the fact that the Jacobian has a key role for computing the

43:32.080 --> 43:40.580
