46:47.820 --> 46:49.780
with each other.

46:49.780 --> 46:54.380
And I have a couple of elements in the off-diagonal elements, which are basically my loop-closer

46:54.380 --> 46:55.380
constraints.

46:55.380 --> 47:01.540
So where a node xi is connected to a node xj, which are not sequential.

47:01.860 --> 47:06.560
So this matrix can be seen as kind of an adjacency matrix of that graph, which tells you what

47:06.560 --> 47:09.140
the connections in your graph actually are.

47:09.140 --> 47:12.420
And the key thing is, this matrix is typically not dense.

47:12.420 --> 47:16.540
It's typically a very sparse matrix, typically the larger the environment even the sparse

47:16.540 --> 47:18.020
of this matrix gets.

47:18.020 --> 47:22.860
It's quite clear from the intuition point of view, if you have for example a robot navigating

47:22.860 --> 47:27.400
